Yes, you are right. In our study, the accelerometers were used in the hip, however now it raises another question:
>From the package documentation, I understood this would be accounted for using the parameters listed below (which I used in the primary analysis).
GGIR would use only the sleep log for participants with available sleep log data, while it would use the HorAngle as the guider for those without a sleep log.
Did I misunderstand it?

    sensor.location = "hip", # Accelerometers were placed in the hip
    relyonguider = T, # Rely primarily on the sleep log, then in HorAngle if sleep log not available
    loglocation = "sleep_log_path.csv", # path to the sleep log
    colid = 1, # Id in the first column of sleep log
    coln1 = 2, # First night in the second column of sleep log
    sleepwindowType = "TimeInBed", # Log was registered as time in bed

That in itself does not explain the duplicated nights yet in your output. So, I started reinserting some of the arguments you used and see that dayborder = 20 causing the duplicated days. My quick impression is that dayborder no longer works for part 5, after the introduction of qwindow to part 5 earlier this year.

Right, I did not suspect this could be causing the trouble. I'll check which alternative processing strategy works best.

I was not using dayborder myself and will have a look whether this can be fixed.

If you find out the answer I'd like to know.

Thank you again for your time and the great resource you built!
Cheers,
Danilo

